{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789944936,"nonce":"B0nHDNUaKh9koNQROiNsT/7uz7c=","title":"ArraySort.sort through a function-typed field is nondeterministic: wrong order, SIGSEGV, or a hang","message":"typedef IComparer\u003cT\u003e = T-\u003eT-\u003eInt;\n    class S { var comparer:IComparer\u003cInt\u003e = S.compare; ...\n      ArraySort.sort(arr, this.comparer);   // [0,3,1] unsorted, or SIGSEGV, or hang\n\nFive runs of the same binary (HEAD 67384b1b, --llvm --release --no-cache) gave\nexit 0 (unsorted), 137, 0, 124, 124. `ArraySort.sort(arr, compare)` with the\nstatic directly returns unsorted deterministically; `(a, b) -\u003e a - b` sorts.\nNondeterminism means a garbage read: the closure built from the field\ninitializer (a typedef'd function type) is probably called through the\ngeneric `cmp` (unannotated -\u003e Dynamic) with the wrong ABI. Conformance\nIssue6722 flips between WRONG_ANSWER and CRASH on it.\n\nIssue1827 is the same class of symptom with a different shape: a static\ninline `call(d1, d2)` with unannotated parameters over `g++` arguments\nreports a different garbage value every run (16, -1728, -12672, -1168) and\nthen exits 139.","files":null}]}